From db8a770c2fb427d52d4a9d911cdbd439415a1424 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anurodh Pokharel <anurodhp@icloud.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:09:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] liblaunch: serialize bare scalar messages -- fixes
 daemon check-in (DAR-153)

launch_data_pack() refused every non-container launch_data_t, which made
the liblaunch check-in handshake structurally impossible: no launchd-managed
daemon could ever check in, and syslogd exit(1)'d within milliseconds of
every single spawn, deterministically, on every boot.

Root cause
----------
Real Apple liblaunch's launch_data_pack()/launch_data_unpack() serialize ANY
launch_data_t -- the wire format is a recursive walk over the launch_data
union, and a bare top-level scalar is a legitimate message. The protocol
genuinely depends on that:

  * the check-in request every managed daemon sends is a bare
    LAUNCH_DATA_STRING -- syslogd.tproj/syslogd.c:305 does
    launch_msg(launch_data_new_string(LAUNCH_KEY_CHECKIN));
  * launchd's own ipc_readmsg() explicitly branches on receiving a bare
    LAUNCH_DATA_STRING;
  * launchd's error replies (launch_data_new_errno()) are bare scalars too,
    so the same defect broke the reply direction.

This PureDarwin reimplementation is built on xpc2nv()/nvlist_pack() instead,
and an nvlist has no representation for a bare scalar at top level --
xpc2nv() hard-asserts unless handed a dictionary or an array. So the function
carried a container-only guard that returned errno=EINVAL/0 for everything
else. The resulting failure chain:

  launch_data_pack() returns 0 (EINVAL)
    -> launchd_msg_send(): `if (lh->sendlen == 0) { errno = ENOMEM; return -1; }`
       -- returns BEFORE ever reaching sendmsg()
    -> launch_msg_internal(): errno != EAGAIN, so `goto out` with resp == NULL
    -> launch_msg() returns NULL
    -> syslogd's launch_config(): "launchd checkin failed" -> exit(1)

Fix
---
Transparently box a top-level scalar in a single-entry dictionary under a
reserved key, and unbox it in launch_data_unpack(). Both peers of this
protocol (clients via liblaunch, launchd itself via ipc.c) run this same
code, so the boxing is symmetric and never escapes the API surface. Unboxing
additionally requires the dictionary to hold exactly that one reserved entry,
so a real single-key message dictionary can never be mistaken for a box.

The change is strictly additive in behaviour: boxing only engages for
messages that previously failed outright, and unboxing only matches a
dictionary the old code could never have produced. No previously working
path changes.

Boxing is allowlisted to the scalar types xpc2nv_primitive() can actually
serialize. It calls xpc_api_misuse() -- which aborts -- for XPC_TYPE_SHMEM,
XPC_TYPE_ERROR and anything unrecognised, and defers XPC_TYPE_FD/CONNECTION/
ENDPOINT to the port_serializer block, which aborts too. The old guard turned
all of those into a graceful EINVAL, and that must not regress into an abort:
this code runs inside PID 1, where dying takes the kernel with it (the same
hazard launch_data_alloc() already documents for LAUNCH_DATA_MACHPORT).
LAUNCH_DATA_FD/MACHPORT/ERRNO are backed by plain int64/uint64 objects here,
so they round-trip through the numeric cases rather than the aborting paths.

Verified on real QEMU boots
--------------------------
Before: every boot ended with
  DAR153-USR: launch_msg(CHECKIN) returned NULL -- exit(1)
  exit_with_reason() pid=3 comm=syslogd rv=256   (rv=256 == exit code 1)

After: sendmsg() is reached for the first time -- fd 11 confirmed a real
socket (fstat mode=0xc1b6), 75-byte packed body, 91 bytes sent -- and both
lines above are gone. Final boot ran its full window with
--expect-fail 'exit_with_reason.*syslogd' and passed: zero syslogd exits.

This also corrects DAR-153's own prior hypothesis that the early exit came
from dispatch_main()/pthread_exit(). It did not; rv=256 was this check-in
failure all along.

Not fixed here (separate, pre-existing): launchd never dispatches the
EVFILT_READ event for the check-in socketpair, so the client now blocks
awaiting a reply instead of exiting. ipc_open() registers fd 10 successfully
(kevent_mod rc=1, errno=0) but ipc_callback() never fires. Filed separately.
