From 5e36a7f2607c7ebb6fdfb24c5aa26966012fd7d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anurodh Pokharel <anurodhp@icloud.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 23:02:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] liblaunch/libxpc: real out-of-band descriptor passing
 (DAR-202)

Fixes the PID-1 abort immediately after DAR-200: every boot, syslogd's
check-in reply killed launchd with

    PureDarwin libxpc misuse: Cannot currently serialize mach ports in
    launch_data_pack()

and the kernel panicked on initproc's death.

Root cause. The abort's wording is misleading -- it is NOT a Mach port.
xpc2nv_primitive() routes XPC_TYPE_FD through the same port_serializer
block as XPC_TYPE_CONNECTION/XPC_TYPE_ENDPOINT, and it is the *fd* case
that fires: job_export(j, for_checkin=true) builds the Sockets entry with
xpc_fd_create() (DAR-153), and syslogd's plist has a real
Sockets/BSDSystemLogger socket. The MachServices entries in that same
reply are int64-backed MACH_PORT_NULL placeholders and serialize fine --
real Apple never transports Mach ports through this socket message
either; the client resolves them itself via bootstrap_check_in() in
launch_msg() -> launch_mach_checkin_service().

Fix, matching real Apple's design: give descriptors real out-of-band
transport, which is what launch_data_pack()'s fd_where/fd_cnt out-params
and launch_data_unpack()'s fds/fd_cnt in-params existed for all along.
Both blocks were hard xpc_api_misuse() aborts; both transports around
them were already complete and wired up (lh->sendfds -> SCM_RIGHTS in
launchd_msg_send(), lh->recvfds <- SCM_RIGHTS in launchd_msg_recv(), and
core.c's job_mig_legacy_ipc_request() fileport array on the MIG path).

  - pack: fileport_makefd() the object's fileport into fd_where[n],
    store index n in the body. Returns -1 (nothing transported) rather
    than aborting for a non-fileport port or a missing/full fd array --
    this runs in PID 1, where aborting panics the kernel.
  - unpack: fileport_makeport() the received descriptor back into the
    XPC_TYPE_FD object so launch_data_get_fd()/xpc_fd_dup() works, and
    report the count consumed via *fdoffset (it was hardcoded 0).

Ownership differs from real Apple's by necessity: our XPC_TYPE_FD stores
a fileport, not the original descriptor, so the serializer must MINT a
descriptor. Those are owned by the caller of launch_data_pack() and are
now closed by both in-tree callers (launchd_msg_send after sendmsg on
every path; job_mig_legacy_ipc_request after fileport_makeport and on
out_bad). launchd_msg_recv likewise closes the raw SCM_RIGHTS descriptors
once unpack has converted them to fileports. Capped at 128 descriptors
per message -- the API passes no length for fd_where, and 128 is the
smallest real caller buffer (core.c's int out_fds[LAUNCHD_MAX_LEGACY_FDS]).

Second, independent bug found and fixed on the way (xpc_dictionary.c):
xpc2nv_primitive() added its five type-tagged inner nvlists
(connection/endpoint/fileport/date/double) with nvlist_add_nvlist(),
which tags the pair NV_TYPE_NVLIST (13) -- and nv2xpc()'s per-pair switch
only handles NV_TYPE_NVLIST_ARRAY (14) and NV_TYPE_NVLIST_DICTIONARY
(15). Every one of those entries was therefore SILENTLY DROPPED on the
receiving side, no error anywhere. Verified with a real pack/unpack round
trip against this tree's own libnv before changing anything. Now added
with nvlist_add_nvlist_dictionary().

Also hardened, both reachable only now that descriptors really flow:
